---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";

const toc = [
  ["install", "Install"],
  ["quick-start", "Quick start"],
  ["tools", "Tools"],
  ["shortcuts", "Keyboard shortcuts"],
  ["mcp", "Agent bridge (MCP)"],
  ["privacy", "Permissions & privacy"],
  ["architecture", "Architecture"],
  ["development", "Development"],
  ["troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"],
];

const shortcuts = [
  ["I", "Inspect: hover for the floating inspector, click to select"],
  ["M", "Measure: distances from the selection (or the parent) to the hovered element"],
  ["R", "Ruler: drag to measure an area, click the rulers to add guides"],
  ["E", "Live edit: edit CSS of the selected element, double-click to edit text"],
  ["K", "Eyedropper: sample any pixel on screen"],
  ["G", "Toggle the layout grid"],
  ["X", "Toggle X-ray outlines on every element"],
  ["O", "Design overlay settings"],
  ["C · T · A · U · P · D", "Colors · Typography · Assets · Audit · Page insights · Changes"],
  ["?", "Show all shortcuts"],
  ["Alt (hold)", "Measure from the selection while inspecting"],
  ["Space", "Pin the selected element's card for side-by-side comparison"],
  ["↑ ↓ ← →", "Select parent, first child, previous or next sibling"],
  ["Shift + drag", "Ruler: constrain to a square and keep previous measurements"],
  ["Alt + drag", "Ruler: disable snapping"],
  ["Esc", "Clear rulers → deselect → close panel → exit"],
];

const tools = [
  ["page_summary", "URL, title, viewport, fonts and main palette of the active tab"],
  ["inspect_element", "Size, position, box model, key computed styles and Tailwind classes for a selector"],
  ["query_elements", "The same for every match of a selector (up to 100)"],
  ["measure_distance", "Edge-to-edge distance between two elements, or inner offsets if one contains the other"],
  ["audit_contrast", "WCAG AA or AAA contrast failures, worst first"],
  ["apply_styles", "Live-edit CSS on the page; returns the running diff"],
  ["set_text", "Replace an element's text to test copy and overflow"],
  ["get_changes", "All live edits as a diff and as plain CSS"],
  ["revert_changes", "Undo every live edit"],
  ["screenshot", "PNG of the visible tab or of a single element"],
  ["bridge_status", "Whether the extension is connected"],
];
---

<Base title="Documentation" description="Install Pixel Lens, learn every tool and shortcut, and connect it to your AI coding agent over MCP.">
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]">
    <aside class="hidden lg:block">
      <nav class="sticky top-24 space-y-1 text-sm" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">On this page</p>
        {
          toc.map(([id, label]) => (
            <a href={`#${id}`} class="block rounded-md px-2 py-1.5 text-ink-300 transition hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white">
              {label}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="prose-docs min-w-0 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">Pixel Lens docs</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-300">
        How to install the extension, what each tool does, and how to connect an AI agent to the page you have open.
      </p>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <p>
        Pixel Lens is not in the Chrome Web Store yet, so for now you build it from source. You need Node.js 22+ and pnpm.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git clone {SITE.github}.git
cd pixel-lens
pnpm install
pnpm --filter @pixel-lens/extension build</code></pre>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code> (or <code>edge://extensions</code>, <code>brave://extensions</code>).</li>
        <li>Turn on <strong>Developer mode</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and choose <code>apps/extension/.output/chrome-mv3</code>.</li>
        <li>Pin Pixel Lens to the toolbar.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>For Firefox (experimental), run <code>pnpm --filter @pixel-lens/extension build:firefox</code> and load the add-on from <code>about:debugging</code>.</p>

      <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open any page and click the Pixel Lens icon, or press <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">P</kbd>.</li>
        <li>Hover elements. The floating card shows size, box model, typography, colors and contrast.</li>
        <li>Click an element to select it, then hold <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> and hover another to see the distance between them.</li>
        <li>Press <kbd class="kbd">?</kbd> for all shortcuts and <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> to leave.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>While an element tool is active, clicks on the page select elements instead of following links or pressing buttons.</p>

      <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
      <h3>Inspect</h3>
      <p>
        The floating card follows your cursor and shows the element's tag and selector, rendered size, a box model diagram (margin, border,
        padding, content), typography, text and background colors with the WCAG contrast ratio, layout properties, borders, shadows and the
        equivalent Tailwind v4 classes. Click to stick it to the selection; <strong>Copy CSS</strong> copies the non-default computed styles as a rule.
        Press <kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd> to pin cards and compare several elements.
      </p>
      <h3>Measure</h3>
      <p>
        Shows edge-to-edge distances between the selected element and the one under the cursor. If one element contains the other you get the
        four inner offsets instead. With nothing selected, the Measure tool measures the hovered element against its parent, which is the
        quickest way to read padding and alignment.
      </p>
      <h3>Ruler & guides</h3>
      <p>
        Drag anywhere to measure a rectangle. Edges snap to nearby elements and guides (hold <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> to disable snapping).
        Click the top or left ruler to drop a guide, drag it to reposition, drag it back onto the ruler to delete it. Distances between
        neighbouring guides are labelled.
      </p>
      <h3>Layout grid & X-ray</h3>
      <p>
        <kbd class="kbd">G</kbd> overlays a centred column grid (columns, gutter, margin, max width and color are configurable in Settings).
        <kbd class="kbd">X</kbd> outlines every element so you can see the page structure at a glance.
      </p>
      <h3>Design overlay</h3>
      <p>
        Paste an image from your clipboard or drop a file onto the page to lay a design export over it. Drag to align, nudge with arrow keys
        (<kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> for 10px), adjust opacity and scale, lock it to click through, and switch on <strong>difference</strong>
        blending: identical pixels turn black, every mismatch lights up. Files named <code>@2x</code> are scaled to 50% automatically.
      </p>
      <h3>Live edit</h3>
      <p>
        Select an element and edit any property in the panel. Press <kbd class="kbd">↑</kbd>/<kbd class="kbd">↓</kbd> in a numeric field to
        nudge by 1 (<kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd> for 10). You can edit the selector the change applies to, add arbitrary properties, hide,
        duplicate or delete elements, and double-click text to rewrite it. Style edits are stored per page and re-applied next time you open
        Pixel Lens there. The Changes panel shows a diff you can copy as a patch, as CSS, or as a ready-made prompt for your coding agent.
      </p>
      <h3>Colors, typography, assets</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Colors</strong>: eyedropper for any pixel, the page palette with usage counts, gradients, and HEX/RGB/HSL/OKLCH output. Export the palette as CSS custom properties.</li>
        <li><strong>Typography</strong>: every font family with its size/line-height/weight combinations. Copy a type scale or a <code>font</code> shorthand.</li>
        <li><strong>Assets</strong>: images, inline SVGs, CSS background images and videos. Download them or copy SVG markup.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Audit & page insights</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Contrast</strong>: scans all visible text against its composited background and lists AA or AAA failures. Click one to select it.</li>
        <li><strong>SEO basics</strong>: title, meta description, single H1, heading order, <code>lang</code>, canonical, viewport, image alt text, Open Graph image.</li>
        <li><strong>Page insights</strong>: transfer size by resource type, detected frameworks, the social preview card and viewport info.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="shortcuts">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {shortcuts.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td class="whitespace-nowrap"><kbd class="kbd">{k}</kbd></td><td>{v}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Shortcuts are ignored while you type in an input or an editable element.</p>

      <h2 id="mcp">Agent bridge (MCP)</h2>
      <p>
        The bridge lets an MCP client such as Claude Code, Cursor or Windsurf work with the tab you have open. It has two parts: the
        <code>@pixel-lens/mcp</code> server, which your agent starts, and the extension, which connects to it over a local WebSocket.
      </p>
      <h3>1. Add the server to your client</h3>
      <pre><code># Claude Code
claude mcp add pixel-lens -- npx -y @pixel-lens/mcp

# Any client that reads an mcpServers config
{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pixel-lens": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@pixel-lens/mcp"] }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>Until the package is on npm, point the client at your local build: <code>node packages/mcp/dist/cli.mjs</code> (run <code>pnpm --filter @pixel-lens/mcp build</code> first).</p>
      <h3>2. Turn on the bridge in the extension</h3>
      <p>
        Open the Pixel Lens options page (right-click the icon → Options, or Settings → Open bridge settings), switch on
        <strong>Agent Bridge</strong> and grant site access. The status dot turns green once the server is running.
      </p>
      <h3>3. Ask your agent</h3>
      <p>"Measure the gap between the logo and the nav, make it 24px, screenshot the header and apply the change to the code."</p>
      <h3>Tools</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {tools.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>Port & security</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The server listens on <code>127.0.0.1:7331</code> only. Change it with <code>--port</code> or <code>PIXEL_LENS_PORT</code>, and set the same port in the options page.</li>
        <li>Connections from web pages are refused: only <code>chrome-extension://</code> and <code>moz-extension://</code> origins are accepted.</li>
        <li>The agent always acts on the active tab of the focused window, so you stay in control of what it sees.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="privacy">Permissions & privacy</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>activeTab</code></td><td>Run on the tab where you click the icon or press the shortcut, and nowhere else.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>scripting</code></td><td>Inject the overlay into that tab on demand.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>storage</code></td><td>Remember your preferences and per-page live edits, locally.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>alarms</code></td><td>Wake the background worker so the agent bridge can reconnect.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>All sites (optional)</td><td>Requested only when you enable the agent bridge, so the agent can read the active tab without a click.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>No analytics, no remote code, no accounts. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="architecture">Architecture</h2>
      <p>The repository is a pnpm monorepo:</p>
      <pre><code>apps/extension   WXT + React browser extension
apps/landing     this site (Astro, static)
packages/core    geometry, color, Tailwind mapping, CSS diff, bridge protocol
packages/mcp     MCP server + WebSocket bridge</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The background worker (<code>entrypoints/background.ts</code>) handles the toolbar click and shortcut, injects the
          overlay with <code>scripting.executeScript</code>, captures screenshots and runs the WebSocket client for the bridge.
        </li>
        <li>
          The overlay (<code>entrypoints/overlay.content</code>) is a runtime-registered content script. Its React UI renders
          into a shadow root so page CSS and overlay CSS never mix. A tiny external store drives the layers (SVG highlights, rulers, grid),
          the inspector card, the dock and the panels.
        </li>
        <li>
          The design overlay renders into a separate light-DOM host: the overlay host is a stacking context, which would
          isolate <code>mix-blend-mode</code> from the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Live edits are written as <code>selector {"{"} prop: value !important {"}"}</code> rules into one
          <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element instead of inline styles, so reverting is trivial and edits behave like real stylesheet changes.
        </li>
        <li>
          The core package has no framework dependencies and is covered by unit tests. The landing page demo imports the same measurement code as the
          extension.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="development">Development</h2>
      <pre><code>pnpm install
pnpm dev:ext        # extension with hot reload (opens a browser)
pnpm dev:web        # this site on localhost:4321
pnpm test           # unit tests (core, mcp)
pnpm --filter @pixel-lens/extension test:e2e   # Playwright, real Chromium
pnpm build          # everything</code></pre>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing happens on click.</strong> Browsers block extensions on <code>chrome://</code> pages, the Web Store and some PDF viewers.</li>
        <li><strong>The eyedropper is missing.</strong> It relies on the EyeDropper API, available in Chromium browsers only.</li>
        <li><strong>Bridge stays yellow.</strong> Make sure your MCP client started the server and that both use the same port. Only one server can own a port.</li>
        <li><strong>Assets won't download.</strong> Cross-origin files without CORS open in a new tab instead; save them from there.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
